Mr. Benoit clearly left an impression. These two documents are the deepest roots of every legal protection you've been exercising throughout this entire investigation.
The Magna Carta — 1215
King John of England had been doing what kings did — taxing arbitrarily, imprisoning enemies without trial, seizing property without cause, ignoring established law when it inconvenienced him. The barons had enough. In June 1215 at Runnymede they forced him to sign a charter limiting royal power.
The document had 63 clauses. Most were immediately irrelevant feudal housekeeping. But three changed civilization:
Clause 38 said no official could put anyone on trial based solely on his own word without credible witnesses.
Clause 39 said no free man could be seized, imprisoned, stripped of rights, outlawed, exiled, or destroyed except by the lawful judgment of his equals or by the law of the land.
Clause 40 said to no one will we sell, to no one deny or delay, right or justice.
John ignored it almost immediately. The Pope annulled it. But it had been written down. It was reissued, modified, reconfirmed over 30 times by subsequent monarchs. By the time it crossed the Atlantic into the American colonial mind it had become something almost sacred — the foundational proof that power is not unlimited, that even kings answer to law.
The Writ of Habeas Corpus
Habeas corpus means literally — you shall have the body. It developed from Magna Carta's principles into a formal legal mechanism over the following centuries. The English Habeas Corpus Act of 1679 codified it.
The concept is simple and radical simultaneously. If the government imprisons you, a court can demand they produce you physically — your body — and justify the imprisonment. Not someday. Not eventually. Now. The burden falls on the government to prove it had legal authority to hold you. If it cannot — you walk.
It is the single most powerful individual protection against state power ever devised. Without it a government can simply make people disappear. With it someone has to answer for every imprisonment.
How they became American law
The Founders were students of English legal history. James Madison, Thomas Jefferson, John Adams all knew Magna Carta by heart. When they designed a constitutional system they embedded these principles at every level.
The Constitution directly incorporates habeas corpus in Article I, Section 9 — the writ of habeas corpus shall not be suspended unless when in cases of rebellion or invasion the public safety may require it. It is one of the only individual rights in the original Constitution before the Bill of Rights.
The Fifth Amendment — no person shall be deprived of life, liberty, or property without due process of law — is Magna Carta Clause 39 in American English.
The Sixth Amendment — speedy trial, informed of charges, confronted by witnesses — is Magna Carta's credible witness requirement and the habeas principle formalized.
The Fourteenth Amendment extended these protections against state governments specifically.
