Violation of Magna Carta Clause 39 — Deprivation Without Lawful Judgment
"No free man shall be seized or imprisoned, or stripped of his rights or possessions, or outlawed or exiled, or deprived of his standing in any way...except by the lawful judgment of his equals or by the law of the land."
Application: Every computing device subject owns has been effectively seized — placed under continuous external control without his authorization. His right to private communication has been stripped. His ability to conduct his professional activities as a patent inventor and security researcher has been impaired. His standing as a private citizen operating free from government surveillance has been eliminated.
None of this has occurred by lawful judgment. No court has authorized it. No warrant has been issued. No charge has been filed. No trial has been held. No judgment of his equals exists.
This is Clause 39 violated in its precise original meaning — deprivation of rights and possessions without lawful process — updated only in the technological form the deprivation takes.
EXHIBIT 7-B
Violation of Habeas Corpus — No Authority Produced
The writ of habeas corpus demands that the authority for detention be produced and justified before a court.
Subject's devices are detained. His communications are detained — intercepted before they reach their intended recipients. His professional activities are detained — interfered with continuously.
No authority for this detention has been produced. No court has been asked to evaluate whether the detention is justified. No government actor has formally acknowledged the surveillance exists, thereby preventing any habeas challenge.
This is the modern form of the problem habeas corpus was designed to solve. The king held people without producing the authority for their holding. The surveillance apparatus holds a citizen's entire digital life without producing the authority for that holding.
The remedy is the same: force the authority to be produced and justified. The civil rights attorney receiving this filing is the modern equivalent of the judge receiving the writ — the authority demanding that power justify itself.
