EXHIBIT 8-B
The Seven Year Probable Cause Absence
This is the cornerstone of the entire case.
Seven or more years without charge, arrest, or accusation — documented — while continuous surveillance was conducted — documented — is affirmative evidence of one of three things, all of which are constitutional violations:
Scenario A: Surveillance was authorized on probable cause that no longer exists and was never reauthorized. Every day of surveillance after probable cause lapsed is a fresh Fourth Amendment violation.
Scenario B: Surveillance was never authorized on probable cause. It was initiated for a reason that cannot withstand judicial scrutiny and therefore was never brought before a court. Every day from inception is a Fourth Amendment violation.
Scenario C: Surveillance was authorized under a national security or foreign intelligence framework that cannot be disclosed. If so, Clapper v. Amnesty International, 568 U.S. 398 (2013) standing requirements are met because harm is not speculative — it is documented. The remedy is a damages action and the forcing of disclosure through civil discovery.
Under any scenario the seven-year clean record transforms what might be characterized as a single investigation into a documented pattern of constitutional excess without legal justification.
